--TEST--
Two `null`s are equal and two `bool`s order, which are ADR 0007 § 4's two non-numeric rows — ADR 0007 § 4, ADR 0090 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `null == null` is the one pair whose static types are both `null`, so
// ADR 0090 § 2's disjointness check passes it and both operands are the
// single value the type has. The answer is therefore a constant, and it is
// PHP's — `var_dump(null == null)` is `true` there too.
echo (null == null) as string, "|\n";
echo (null != null) as string, "|\n";

// The same question one indirection out: a `?string` holding `null` is a
// *tagged* value, so `$a == null` is a tag test rather than this row, and
// the two spellings have to agree.
?string $a = null;
?string $b = null;
echo ($a == null) as string, "|\n";
echo ($a == $b) as string, "|\n";
echo ($a != $b) as string, "|\n";

// A `bool` is the one non-numeric type ADR 0007 § 4's ordering row admits:
// `false < true` is the ordering of the one bit it already is, needs no
// conversion to be exact, and is PHP's answer as well. Every other operand
// with no row is refused where it is written — see
// `the-relational-operators-refuse-every-operand-with-no-ordering-row.nvst`.
bool $t = true;
bool $f = false;
echo ($f < $t) as string, "|\n";
echo ($t < $f) as string, "|\n";
echo ($t >= $t) as string, "|\n";
--EXPECT--
1|
|
1|
1|
|
1|
|
1|
